"Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains (https://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/~par31/preprints/fullchains.pdf)\nciteturn7search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains ... than just maximal within the set system. ... We conjecture ... quantitative extension of Sperner’s theorem: the minimum number of compara-\nSet Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains\nJ. Robert Johnson∗, Imre Leader†and Paul Russell†\nSeptember 18, 2013\nThe purpose of this note is to raise an extremal question on set systems\n(that is, subsets of the power set P(X), where X = {1, 2, . . . , n}) which seems\nto be natural and appealing. Our question is: which set systems of a given\nsize maximise the number of (n + 1)-element chains in P(X)? We refer to these\nchains as maximal chains – we emphasise that they are maximal in P(X) rather\nthan just maximal within the set system.\nQuestion 1. For given |A|, which A ⊆P(X) contains the most maximal\nchains?\nFor A ⊆P(X) we denote the number of maximal chains in A by c(A). We\nwill show that for each ﬁxed α > 0 we have\nmax{c(A) : |A| = α2n} = (α + o(1)) n!\nFor smaller set systems we are unable to answer the question. We conjecture\nthat a ‘tower of cubes’ construction (deﬁned later) is extremal. We ﬁnish by\nmentioning brieﬂy a connection to an extremal problem on posets and a variant\nof our question for the grid graph.\nThis question does not appear to have been asked before, although there has\nbeen work on the problem of maximising the number of k-element chains for\nsmaller k. For k = 2 (that is, maximising the number of comparable pairs) Alon\nand Frankl [1] proved that a tower of cubes is approximately extremal. They\nalso proved a similar but less exact result for arbitrary k (with k ﬁxed and n\nlarge). By contrast, in this note we are interested in the case when k is as large\nas it can be.\nWe note brieﬂy that the question of minimising the number of k-element\nchains has also been studied. In this direction Kleitman [5] proved the following\nquantitative extension of Sperner’s theorem: the minimum number of compara-\nble pairs is achieved by a union of middle layers of P(X). He also conjectured\nthat the same construction minimises the number of k element chains for any k\n– this problem remains open. See also the recent work of Dove, Griggs, Kang\nand Sereni [3].\n∗School of Mathematical Sciences, Queen Mary University of London, London E1 4NS\n†Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics, Centre for Mathematical\nSciences, Wilberforce Road, Cambridge CB3 0WB\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSet Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains (https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4643)\nciteturn7academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Title: Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains ... The purpose of this short problem paper is to raise an extremal question on set systems which seems to be natural and appealing. ... We conjecture that a `tower of cubes' construction is extremal.\nTitle: Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains\nAuthors: J. Robert Johnson, Imre Leader, Paul A. Russell\nDate: Wed Sep 18 13:47:48 2013\n\nThe purpose of this short problem paper is to raise an extremal question on set systems which seems to be natural and appealing. Our question is: which set systems of a given size maximise the number of $(n+1)$-element chains in the power set $\\mathcal{P}(\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\})$? We will show that for each fixed $\\alpha>0$ there is a family of $\\alpha 2^n$ sets containing $(\\alpha+o(1))n!$ such chains, and that this is asymptotically best possible. For smaller set systems we are unable to answer the question. We conjecture that a `tower of cubes' construction is extremal. We finish by mentioning briefly a connection to an extremal problem on posets and a variant of our question for the grid graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Nase conjecture on triple cumulative edge bounds in simple graph drawings — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/326856/the-nase-conjecture-on-triple-cumulative-edge-bounds-in-simp)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The Nase conjecture on triple cumulative edge bounds in simple graph drawings ... Here $E_i(D)$E i​(D) denotes the number of $i$i-edges, and the cumulative notation is obtained by summing the corresponding lower-order edge counts. ... source: Martin Balko, Radoslav Fulek and Jan Kynčl, “Crossing numbers and combinatorial characterization of monotone drawings of K_n”, arXiv:1312.3679 (2014).\n\nThe Nase conjecture on triple cumulative edge bounds in simple graph drawings\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $D$D be a simple drawing of a graph, and let $E_{{\\le}{\\le}{\\le}k}(D)$E≤≤≤k​(D) denote the number of its $\\le\\!\\le\\!\\le k$≤≤≤k-edges, defined by\n\n$$E_{{\\le}{\\le}{\\le}k}(D)=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}E_{{\\le}{\\le}j}(D)=\\sum_{i=0}^{k}{k+2-i\\choose 2}E_i(D).$$E≤≤≤k​(D)=j=0∑k​E≤≤j​(D)=i=0∑k​(2 k+2−i​)E i​(D).\n\nHere $E_i(D)$E i​(D) denotes the number of $i$i-edges, and the cumulative notation is obtained by summing the corresponding lower-order edge counts. The Nase conjecture. If $n\\ge 3$n≥3 and $D$D is a simple drawing of $K_n$K n​, then for every $k$k satisfying $0\\le k<n/2-1$0≤k<n/2−1,\n\n$$E_{{\\le}{\\le}{\\le}k}(D)\\ge 3{k+4\\choose 4}.$$E≤≤≤k​(D)≥3(4 k+4​).\n\nThe conjecture is stronger than Hill's conjecture. It is implied by the paper's theorem for simple $x$x-monotone drawings, and all examples considered by the authors satisfy it; its validity for arbitrary simple drawings remains open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Martin Balko, Radoslav Fulek and Jan Kynčl, “Crossing numbers and combinatorial characterization of monotone drawings of K_n”, arXiv:1312.3679 (2014).\n\ncomplete-graphscrossing-numbersgraph-drawings\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSet Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1309.4643)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains ... We conjecture that a `tower of cubes' construction is extremal. ... @misc{johnson2013setsystemscontainingmaximal, title={Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains}, author={J.\nSubmitted 18 Sept 2013\n\n# Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains\n\nJR\n\nJ. Robert Johnson\n\nIL\n\nImre Leader\n\nPA\n\nPaul A. Russell\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe purpose of this short problem paper is to raise an extremal question on set systems which seems to be natural and appealing. Our question is: which set systems of a given size maximise the number of $(n+1)$(n+1)-element chains in the power set $\\mathcal{P}(\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\})$P({1,2,…,n})? We will show that for each fixed $\\alpha>0$α>0 there is a family of $\\alpha 2^n$α 2 n sets containing $(\\alpha+o(1))n!$(α+o(1))n! such chains, and that this is asymptotically best possible. For smaller set systems we are unable to answer the question. We conjecture that a `tower of cubes' construction is extremal. We finish by mentioning briefly a connection to an extremal problem on posets and a variant of our question for the grid graph.\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{johnson2013setsystemscontainingmaximal, title={Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains}, author={J. Robert Johnson and Imre Leader and Paul A. Russell}, year={2013}, eprint={1309.4643}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1017/S0963548314000510}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4643}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRAINBOW INDUCED SUBGRAPHS IN REPLICATION GRAPHS (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.ed